     9	## 1. What a client gets
    10	
    11	The client sends bytes. Tessera returns a **receipt**: a bundle that anyone can check later, without Tessera
    12	running and without our help.
    13	
    14	## 2. Guarantees
    15	
    16	A checker who has the receipt, the published verifier and its trust configuration can establish:
    17	
    18	| # | Guarantee | Source |
    19	|---|---|---|
    20	| G1 | **Exact bytes.** The receipt binds these exact bytes. Change one bit and the check fails. | C1 |
    21	| G2 | **Presented to Tessera.** The bytes were presented to the Tessera V1 service at some issuance. | C2 |
    22	| G3 | **Both signatures.** Both of the service's keys signed. A missing, stripped or swapped signature fails. | C3 |
    23	| G4 | **The keys were Tessera's.** Two channels named the keys for the service's domain: DNS (`_tessera-v1.wamason.com`, DNSSEC) and a signed git commit. The git commit was in the Bitcoin chain at least 6 blocks before the receipt. | C4, A10.6 |
    24	| G5 | **Existed by a block.** The signed bytes and both signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block, confirmed 6 deep. | C5, A10.1 |
    25	| G6 | **A real-time bound, conditionally.** If assumption A-UTC holds, they existed before a UTC time U (the latest of the 6 confirming blocks' times, plus 2 hours). | A10.1.1 |
    26	| G7 | **Window.** The declared issue time is not before the keys' window opened, and the chain time is not after it closed. | C4, A10.2 |
    27	| G8 | **No Tessera needed.** The verdict depends only on the bundle, the verifier and its trust configuration. | C6 |
    28	| G9 | **An honest verdict.** One of `VALID_STRICT`, `INVALID`, `UNVERIFIABLE`, with a reason code, always with `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED` and `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. When something cannot be established, the verifier says so and does not accept. | C7 |
    29	| G10 | **Honest receipts survive a later capture.** If an attacker later takes over the DNS and git authority records, receipts the service's keys signed honestly still verify, and cannot be moved to other keys or bytes. With the service-key listing, a checker using an authentic verifier release also rejects the attacker's own new keys. A10.4 lets the listing be cut for schedule reasons; G10 then keeps its first sentence only. | A10.4, A10.4.1 (row 33) |
